<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Mycale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/479-bce">479 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Mycale was fought in 479 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a> on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>. A Greek force under the Spartan king Leotychidas and the Athenian Xanthippus landed and destroyed a Persian army and its beached fleet, traditionally on the same day as the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. It ended the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> and opened the liberation of Ionia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale.jpg" alt="Battle of Mycale" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro Lassouras · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Priene_colline_colonne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Greek naval victory at Salamis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> with a minor portion of his army and left his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> to finish the campaign the following year. The Persian fleet was stationed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> and prevent an Ionian revolt. Its morale was breaking, and its commanders waited anxiously for news of Mardonius's land army.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives 300 ships and 60,000 soldiers for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, but Charles Hignett and others think both figures far too high, and estimates of the Persian army range from about 4,000 to 10,000 men. The Greek fleet is usually put at about 110 ships, with perhaps 3,300 marines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> learned that the Greek fleet was approaching, they sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> to Mycale on the mainland, possibly because they judged a sea battle hopeless. They beached their ships behind rocks, built a palisaded camp of cut wood with stakes on the ramparts, and expected a siege.</p><p>The Greek commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leotychidas-ii">Leotychides</a> decided to attack anyway and landed the fleet's marines. The Athenians and Corinthians formed the left wing along the shore, while the Spartans and others were still crossing the hills on the right. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> came out and set up a shield wall. The Greek left engaged first, and the right wing broke the wall by shoving. Tigranes and Mardontes were killed. The Persians fled to their camp, the Ionian contingents defected, and the ethnic Persian troops who held out were attacked in the rear when the Greek left outflanked the camp.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and Mycale the second Persian invasion of Greece was over. The Greek fleet sailed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> to break down the pontoon bridges, found them already destroyed, and the Peloponnesians went home. The Athenians stayed and besieged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at Sestos, which fell after a long siege and began a new phase of the war, the Greek counterattack. Chios, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> joined the Hellenic alliance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia broke away from the Persians.</p><p>Some historians treat the fighting itself as minor. George Cawkwell called it a very minor affair whose results were major because it led to an Ionian revolt. The ancient accounts also differ on the ships: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> has them burned, and Iain McDougall argues that the burned vessels were mostly Ionian and too damaged to salvage. Spoils from the campaign were displayed at Delphi and, according to Vitruvius, Persian masts and spars were used in the roof of the Odeon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Mycale” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Battle of Mycale</b> in 479 BCE Chios joined the Greek alliance and then the Delian League, providing ships rather than tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ionian Revolt and two Persian invasions of Greece, the Allies defeated the forces of Xerxes I at the Battle of Plataea and the <b>Battle of Mycale</b> in 479 BC, then took Sestos and, in 478 BC, Byzantion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Battle of Mycale</b> in 479 BCE Samos regained its freedom, but the crushing defeat by Athens in 439 BCE cost it its walls, its fleet and a heavy indemnity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After victories at Salamis (480 BCE) and Plataea and <b>Mycale</b> (479 BCE), the Greeks carried the war to the Aegean.</blockquote><a href="/state/hellenic-alliance">Hellenic Alliance</a></li></ul></section>
